\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Visualisation of the exponentially truncated power law probability mass function on log-log axes under different parameter combinations. Each point represents a discrete value and corresponding probability mass, with a line connecting sequential points for illustration purposes. The left plot shows the effect of varying $\tau $ while keeping $x_{max}$ and $c$ constant, which results in changes to the slope of the linear part of the connecting line. The central plot shows the effect of varying $x_{max}$ while keeping $\tau $ and $c$ constant, which results in vertical translation of the connecting curve, with different support for $x$. The right plot shows the effect of increasing $c$ while keeping $\tau $ and $x_{max}$ constant, which results in later decay. The orange line is identical in all plots, corresponding to parameters $\tau = 0.5$, $x_{max} = 100$, and $c = 200$.}}{15}{figure.caption.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:exp_powerlaw_params}{{4.1}{15}{Visualisation of the exponentially truncated power law probability mass function on log-log axes under different parameter combinations. Each point represents a discrete value and corresponding probability mass, with a line connecting sequential points for illustration purposes. The left plot shows the effect of varying $\tau $ while keeping $x_{max}$ and $c$ constant, which results in changes to the slope of the linear part of the connecting line. The central plot shows the effect of varying $x_{max}$ while keeping $\tau $ and $c$ constant, which results in vertical translation of the connecting curve, with different support for $x$. The right plot shows the effect of increasing $c$ while keeping $\tau $ and $x_{max}$ constant, which results in later decay. The orange line is identical in all plots, corresponding to parameters $\tau = 0.5$, $x_{max} = 100$, and $c = 200$}{figure.caption.10}{}}
